<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mértola</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Myrtilis, Myrtilis Iulia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mértola is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alentejo">Alentejo</a> region of southern Portugal, on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a>. As Myrtilis Iulia it was a river port of Roman Lusitania, where in 440 the Suebic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechila">Rechila</a> forced the Roman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/censorius">Censorius</a> to surrender. Under Muslim rule it was for a time the capital of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, and its mosque, later converted into a church, survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mertola.jpg" alt="Mértola" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexey Komarov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%A9rtola,_Portugal_2019-11-10-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mértola stands on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> in the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alentejo">Alentejo</a>, near the Spanish border, at the northernmost navigable part of the river. Its municipality had 6,206 inhabitants in 2021, and the town itself around 2,500. The site made it a river port: farm produce and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> from the lower Alentejo went down the Guadiana to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The place was inhabited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> at least, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> renamed Myrtilis, its pre-Roman name, Myrtilis Iulia. In the 1st and 2nd centuries it was a commercial centre of the Pacensis region and minted its own coin, and it became a municipium in the time of Augustus. In the Migration Period Sueves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> invaded; trade shrank but continued, as Greek tombstones of the 6th and 7th centuries and Byzantine merchants suggest.</p><p>From about 711 Mértola belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> for over four hundred years. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1031 it became an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, until the taifa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> took it in 1044-1045. Between 1144 and 1150 it was again the seat of an independent state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-qasi">Ibn Qasi</a>, a mystic and military leader who united southern Portugal and fought the Almoravides; an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> army ended it.</p><p>King Sancho II of Portugal took the town in 1238 and gave it to the Order of St James, whose seat was there until 1316. Its economic weight faded, revived briefly in the 15th and 16th centuries when it supplied Portuguese troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> with cereals, and revived again with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mined at São Domingos from about 1850 until 1965.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The castle has a 30-metre keep, finished around 1292, with an inner hall covered with Gothic vaulting. The main church was built as a mosque in the 12th and 13th centuries and converted after 1238; it keeps four naves, a mihrab niche and horseshoe-arched portals, and is the only medieval mosque to survive in Portugal. The museum holds the most important nucleus of Islamic art in Portugal. In 2017 the town began the process of seeking UNESCO World Heritage status.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mértola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 439 their king Rechila entered Emerita, and the following year he forced the Roman commander Censorius to surrender at <b>Myrtilis</b> (Mértola), effectively ending imperial control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1144, as Almoravid rule weakened, his followers seized <b>Mértola</b>, and he proclaimed himself imam and mahdi.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-taifa-of-mertola">Second Taifa of Mértola</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1033 a local leader, known as Ibn Tayfur, made <b>Mértola</b> and its district independent, in the period when the small principalities of the south-west were breaking away from both the Hammudids and the Taifa of Badajoz.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-mertola">Taifa of Mértola</a></li></ul></section>
